# Summary
Introduced an availability bug by enabling the WAL-backed durable process without bounding, compacting, or incrementally tracking WAL length. The problematic primitive existed before, but this commit made it part of the live durable control-plane path and added the full-WAL scan on checkpoint.
The new WAL-backed constructors are now used by the control-plane process. Each Raft log-store mutation appends another WAL frame, and control-plane commands checkpoint the durable restart artifact before returning. During checkpoint capture, the new wal_replay_offset is computed with wal.clean_len(), which calls read_records() and read_records_from(0). That implementation reads the entire WAL file into memory and decodes all frames from the beginning just to find the clean length. The commit does not add any compaction or truncation of checkpointed WAL bytes; truncation is only used for torn tails during replay. As a result, repeated control-plane mutations such as storage-node heartbeat updates can grow the WAL without bound, and every later checkpoint performs O(total WAL size) disk I/O, allocation, checksum work, and frame decoding while holding the log-store lock. A low-privileged local/internal actor that can send control-plane traffic can therefore consume disk and CPU/memory until the experimental control plane becomes unavailable.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit 855bd471 (`Bound durable Raft peer RPC WAL acknowledgements`).

The durable process path now keeps ordinary Raft peer RPC responses on the checkpoint-before-response boundary and compacts the acknowledged WAL suffix before releasing the response. The default durable peer RPC context checkpoints append/vote state into the restart artifact, and the regression verifies that a vote response is returned only after the durable artifact contains the vote and the WAL clean length has been compacted back to the base offset. WAL-only replay remains covered only for the pre-response crash case: if the process exits after WAL fsync but before checkpoint/ack, artifact-plus-WAL recovery still restores the mutation.

Phase 12.4 plan text was also corrected to remove the stale WAL-only acknowledgement direction and record the bounded checkpoint/compaction contract for ordinary durable peer RPCs.

