## Report
Validated the finding against commit afb03c8d085652bdb051b658f429c6d410503f95. Code review confirmed the live experimental control-plane now uses WAL-backed durable constructors in crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2205-2225. Control-plane command submission checkpoints before returning at crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1555-1568, and node heartbeat refreshes submit a repeatable Raft command at crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1620-1644. On the storage side, checkpoint capture calls ControlPlaneRaftRestartArtifact::capture() -> export_restart_artifact_with_wal_replay_offset() at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4935-4944, which locks the log store and calls wal.clean_len() at lines 4105-4120. WAL mutation appends a new length-prefixed frame to the file with OpenOptions::append and fsync at lines 4728-4787. clean_len() calls read_records() -> read_records_from(0) at lines 4814-4821; read_records_from opens the WAL and read_to_end()s the whole file into a Vec at lines 4825-4852, then loops over all frames decoding and validating them at lines 4864-4910. The only truncation found is for a torn tail during replay at lines 4789-4812, not checkpoint compaction.

Dynamic PoC: I built /workspace/argmin-validation-poc, which writes valid ControlPlaneRaftWalFrame SaveVote records, restores a WAL-backed ControlPlaneRaftLogStore, then calls ControlPlaneRaftRestartArtifact::capture(). Scaling output from artifacts/logs/scaling.log shows checkpoint time growing with total WAL bytes while the durable artifact size stays constant: 2.12 MB WAL -> capture 16/7 ms; 21.2 MB WAL -> capture 186/76 ms; 106 MB WAL -> capture 779/757 ms. This reproduces the O(total WAL size) checkpoint behavior.

Availability impact was also demonstrated with a bounded memory limit: `(ulimit -v 80000; ... 50000 2048 1)` produced `Error: Io { context: "read control-plane OpenRaft WAL", source: Kind(OutOfMemory) }`, showing the full-WAL allocation can make durable restore/checkpoint operations fail under memory pressure.

Debugger validation: gdb/rust-gdb was unavailable because gdb is not installed, but rust-lldb succeeded. The breakpoint at `storage::control_plane_raft::ControlPlaneRaftWalFile::read_records_from` first hit replay, then after continuing hit the checkpoint path with stack: read_records_from(control_plane_raft.rs:4826) -> read_records(control_plane_raft.rs:4815) -> clean_len(control_plane_raft.rs:4819) -> export_restart_artifact_with_wal_replay_offset(control_plane_raft.rs:4111) -> ControlPlaneRaftRestartArtifact::capture(control_plane_raft.rs:4943) -> PoC main. This confirms the suspected full-WAL scan is on checkpoint capture.

Crash/valgrind attempts: no memory-safety crash is expected for this availability bug. A direct resource-exhaustion attempt under ulimit caused a controlled OutOfMemory I/O error rather than a process crash. valgrind is not installed in the container (`bash: valgrind: command not found`). LLDB was used for the required debugger trace.

