# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: low | Policy adjusted: low
## Rationale
Keep medium. The code evidence supports the core bug: WAL-backed control-plane mutations append frames, and each checkpoint computes clean_len by rereading and decoding the entire WAL from offset 0. The path is reachable through normal internal control-plane RPC such as RefreshNodeHeartbeat, and validation evidence demonstrated O(total WAL size) checkpoint scaling plus memory-pressure failure. However, this is not public network exposure: it requires experimental Raft mode and access to a private Unix control-plane socket or a configured storage-node role. The impact is availability of the control plane, not C/I, auth bypass, RCE, or credential compromise, so high/critical would be overstated.
## Likelihood
medium - Exploitation is straightforward once the attacker can send control-plane traffic, but the vulnerable endpoint is a private Unix socket in an experimental, opt-in control-plane mode rather than an internet-exposed S3 API. Filesystem permissions and deployment topology are meaningful preconditions.
## Impact
medium - Repeated accepted control-plane mutations can grow the WAL without bound and make later checkpoints perform full-file reads, allocation, and decoding while checkpointing. This can exhaust disk or cause sustained CPU/memory/I/O pressure, making the experimental control-plane unavailable. No confidentiality, integrity, code execution, or identity compromise was identified.
## Assumptions
- The affected deployment has ARGMIN_PROCESS_ROLE=control-plane and ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT=true.
- The attacker is an internal/local actor able to connect to the configured control-plane Unix socket, or controls/compromises a configured storage-node component that legitimately sends heartbeats.
- Filesystem permissions on the control-plane socket directory are the main access boundary; the repository does not show a separate application-level authentication mechanism for control-plane Unix RPC.
- experimental Raft control-plane mode enabled
- control-plane state path and Unix socket configured
- attacker can send accepted control-plane RPCs such as RefreshNodeHeartbeat
- repeated log-store mutations are generated over time
## Path
actor/storage-node -> Unix control-plane RPC -> RefreshNodeHeartbeat -> Raft command -> WAL append + checkpoint -> clean_len full WAL scan -> control-plane DoS
