## Narrative
This is a real availability bug in an in-scope but non-default experimental control-plane runtime path. The argmin-s3 binary supports a control-plane process role and an experimental Raft mode. In that mode the constructor uses WAL-backed durable Raft state. The Unix RPC handler accepts RefreshNodeHeartbeat and dispatches it into ExperimentalRaftControlPlane::refresh_node_heartbeat, which submits a Raft command. submit_raft_command checkpoints the durable restart artifact before returning. On the storage side, checkpoint capture calls export_restart_artifact_with_wal_replay_offset, which calls wal.clean_len(); clean_len calls read_records_from(0), reads the whole WAL into a Vec, then decodes and validates every frame. WAL mutation appends a new fsynced frame, and no checkpoint compaction/truncation was found except torn-tail truncation during replay. Earlier validation included an executable PoC showing checkpoint time increasing with total WAL size and an OutOfMemory error under a bounded memory limit. Severity remains medium: impact can be significant control-plane availability loss, but reachability is internal/local, requires experimental mode, and does not affect confidentiality, integrity, or identity.
## Controls
- ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT flag defaults false
- control-plane role requires explicit state and Unix socket paths
- control-plane socket directory must be owned by the process uid and not group/world accessible
- Unix RPC worker limit of 64
- control-plane RPC read/write timeouts
- not reachable from the public S3 HTTP listener
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests or IaC were found in the repository to prove how often the experimental control-plane is enabled in real deployments.
- Static review cannot verify runtime filesystem ownership/mode choices beyond the code-enforced private socket directory checks.
- The executable validation PoC exercised the WAL/checkpoint behavior directly; the end-to-end Unix RPC attack path is inferred from static dispatch evidence.
- Operational mitigations such as systemd sandboxing, disk quotas, cgroups, or external process supervision are not visible in the repository.
